conomic decisions, particularly in the context of inflation and monetary policy.\n\nFriedman also made significant advancements in the theory of consumption, particularly through his formulation of the Permanent Income Hypothesis (PIH). This hypothesis asserts that an individual's consumption choices are determined not just by current income but by their expected long-term average income. The formula can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ C_t = \\alpha Y_t + (1 - \\alpha) C_{t-1} \\]\n\nwhere \\( C_t \\) is current consumption, \\( Y_t \\) is current income, and \\( \\alpha \\) is a parameter reflecting the degree of adjustment to changes in income. This model highlights the importance of understanding consumer behavior over time and has profound implications for fiscal and monetary policy, as it suggests that temporary changes in income will have a limited effect on consumption.\n\nFriedman's work on monetary policy emphasizes the significance of controlling the money supply to manage economic stability. He argued that fluctuations in the money supply are a primary driver of economic cycles, advocating for a steady, predictable increase in the money supply to promote economic growth and stability. This perspective laid the groundwork for the monetarist school of thought, which contrasts with Keynesian economics that focuses on fiscal policy as a tool for economic management.\n\nFriedman's theories have been influential in shaping economic policy and have sparked extensive debate regarding the effectiveness of monetary versus fiscal policy. His emphasis on empirical validation and the role of expectations has led to a deeper understanding of economic dynamics and the complexities of consumer behavior.\n\nIn summary, Milton Friedman’s contributions to economics encompass a robust methodology for positive economics, the adaptive expectations hypothesis, and the Permanent Income Hypothesis, all of which underscore the interplay between expectations, consumption, and monetary poli